EXPORT(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     EXPORT(1)

NAME
    export — Package the entire workspace into an archive, because
             eventually someone will ask, "Can you just send me everything?"

SYNOPSIS
    export <zip|tar|tar.gz>
    export [name] <zip|tar|tar.gz>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'export' command creates an archive containing the contents of
    the CLI workspace and saves it under /workspace/exports.

    It supports ZIP, TAR, and GZIP-compressed TAR archives.

    You can either let the command invent a timestamped filename or
    provide your own name.

    In short:

        export zip
        export my_backup zip
        export project.tar.gz tar.gz

    Because manually collecting 847 files into a ZIP is a hobby
    nobody asked for.

ARGUMENTS
    name
        Optional name for the generated archive.

        If provided, any trailing supported archive extension is
        removed before the selected format is added.

        For example:

            export backup.zip tar

        produces:

            backup.tar

    format
        Required archive format.

        Supported formats are:

            zip
            .zip
            tar
            .tar
            gz
            tgz
            .tgz
            tar.gz
            .tar.gz

        The formats 'gz' and 'tgz' are normalized to '.tar.gz'.

DEFAULT NAME
    If no name is provided, the archive receives a timestamped name:

        data_YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.<format>

    For example:

        data_20260903-144210.zip

    Because naming files manually is apparently another problem
    that timestamps can solve.

OUTPUT
    All exported archives are written to:

        /workspace/exports

    The directory is created automatically if it does not already
    exist.

ARCHIVE CONTENTS
    ZIP
        Every file in the workspace is recursively added to the ZIP.

        Files are stored using paths relative to the workspace root.

    TAR
        The entire workspace is added recursively to the TAR archive
        using the workspace directory's name as the archive root.

    TAR.GZ
        Same contents as TAR, but compressed with gzip.

BEHAVIOR
    The command accepts both an older one-argument syntax and a newer
    two-argument syntax:

        export zip
            Creates a timestamped ZIP archive.

        export my_backup zip
            Creates 'my_backup.zip'.

    Format matching is case-insensitive.

    If an unsupported format is supplied, the command prints:

        Unsupported format. Use zip, tar, or tar.gz

    The workspace is then left safely unexported.

ERRORS
    Usage: export [name] <zip|tar|tar.gz>
        No arguments were supplied.

    Unsupported format. Use zip, tar, or tar.gz
        The requested archive format is not supported.

    Export error: <error>
        Something went wrong while creating the archive.

        The filesystem has once again chosen violence.

SUCCESS
    On success, the command prints:

        Workspace exported to <path>

    At which point your entire workspace has been carefully stuffed
    into an archive and deposited in /workspace/exports.

EXAMPLES
    > export zip
        Creates something like:

            /workspace/exports/data_20260903-144210.zip

        The timestamp handles the naming crisis for you.

    > export backup zip
        Creates:

            /workspace/exports/backup.zip

    > export project.tar.gz tar.gz
        Creates:

            /workspace/exports/project.tar.gz

        A compressed archive with a name that already knows exactly
        what it is.

    > export project.zip tar
        Creates:

            /workspace/exports/project.tar

        The supplied archive extension is removed and replaced with
        the requested format.

FUN FACTS
    - ZIP archives use DEFLATE compression.
    - TAR archives are uncompressed.
    - TAR.GZ archives use gzip compression.
    - ZIP exports contain workspace files using paths relative to
      the workspace.
    - TAR exports include the workspace directory itself as the
      archive root.
    - The export directory is created automatically.
    - Format names are case-insensitive.
    - The command supports both the old one-argument syntax and the
      newer named syntax.
    - Nothing says "backup strategy" quite like naming your archive
      'data_20260903-144210.zip' and hoping for the best.

